# WealthWise ML-Backend — Ollama Performance TODO

**Decision:** Switch to a **1B model** (e.g. `llama3.2:1b`) for all agent LLM calls, with **thinking mode disabled**. CPU inference is fixed at ~5 tok/s regardless of model between 1B–4B in this environment, so the primary lever is *token count per call*, not further model comparison. A 1B model should also reduce per-token latency somewhat and has a much smaller memory/context footprint, which helps concurrent-request behavior.

**Benchmark (llama3.2:1b, Q4_K_M, local CPU):**
- Filter extraction: 12.80s, 8.77 tok/s (prompt: 89 tok, output: 20 tok)
- Response generation: 18.65s, 9.45 tok/s (prompt: 123 tok, output: 131 tok)

---

## P0 — Model swap & config (do first, blocks everything else)

- [x] Pull `llama3.2:1b` (or your preferred 1B model) and benchmark it the same way as the 3B/4B models — same prompts, record tokens/sec, so you have a real baseline before rewiring agents onto it
- [ ] Explicitly disable thinking mode on every Ollama request: pass `think: false` in the `/api/chat` or `/api/generate` payload (or set it in a Modelfile if using a model that defaults to reasoning mode)
- [ ] Verify thinking is actually off by inspecting raw responses for `<think>` blocks — don't just trust the flag, confirm empirically
- [x] Update `app/ollama.py` `generate()` to accept model name as a parameter (not hardcoded) so different agents can request different models if needed later
- [x] Set explicit `num_predict` per call site instead of a single global default — see per-agent table below
- [x] Add a hard client-side timeout to every Ollama call, computed as `(num_predict / measured_tok_per_sec) * 1.5` — don't use one blanket timeout for all agents since output length varies

## P0 — Re-benchmark after swap

- [x] Re-run the same benchmark matrix (short prompt / long prompt, various `num_predict`) on `llama3.2:1b` with thinking off
- [x] Confirm `_extract_transaction_filters`-style short JSON tasks now complete in single-digit seconds
- [x] Confirm no timeout on previously-failing long-prompt cases (the qwen3:4b >180s case)
- [x] Document the new tok/s number — this becomes the constant your timeout math is based on

## P1 — Remove Ollama from the search critical path

- [x] Implement rule-based/regex extraction in `_extract_transaction_filters` (tools.py:24-46) for common patterns: amount comparisons (`over $50`, `under $20`), date ranges (`last month`, `this week`), known category/merchant keywords
- [x] Only fall back to the 1B model when rule-based parsing finds nothing usable
- [x] Cap `num_predict` for filter-extraction Ollama calls to ~40-60 tokens (it's just a small JSON object)
- [x] Apply the same rule-based-first approach to `query_transactions_dynamic` (tools.py:263-387), which has a larger filter schema (query_type, merchant, date, amount_min/max, etc.)

## P1 — Per-agent token budgets

Set explicit `num_predict` ceilings per call site rather than reusing 256 everywhere:

| Agent / function | Task | `num_predict` |
|---|---|---|
| `_extract_transaction_filters` | JSON filter extraction | 60 |
| `query_transactions_dynamic` | JSON filter extraction (larger schema) | 100 |
| `answer_alerts_question` | Short analysis | 150 |
| `explain_chart_or_alert` | Short explanation | 150 |
| `answer_goal_question` | Goal answer | 200 |
| `answer_budget_question` | Budget analysis | 200 |
| `answer_insights_question` | Insights answer | 200 |
| `generate_report_narrative` | Full report narrative | 256 |
| `classify_intent` | Intent label only | 20 |
| `answer_database_question` | Schema Q&A | 100 |

- [x] Apply these caps in code
- [x] Recalculate each call site's timeout using the new per-task token budget and the 1B model's measured tok/s

## P1 — Fix the auth bug (independent of Ollama work)

- [x] Trace why `/debug/traces` requires auth despite `/debug` being marked public in `middleware.py:91-141` — check path-prefix matching logic and whether the traces router declares its own auth dependency that overrides the middleware's public list
  - **Root cause:** `app/routers/debug.py` had `user_id: str = Depends(get_user_id)` on `/traces`, `/events`, and `/test/flow`, which overrode the middleware's public `/debug` prefix.
  - **Fix:** Removed `Depends(get_user_id)` from all debug router endpoints. Removed unused imports.

## P2 — Reduce load on a single CPU worker

- [x] Do **not** raise `OLLAMA_NUM_PARALLEL` above 1 on CPU — concurrent generations on fixed cores just divide the same total throughput and make each individual call slower and less predictable
  - **Done:** Added `OLLAMA_NUM_PARALLEL` env var (default 1) and `asyncio.Semaphore` in `app/ollama.py`.
- [x] Add a simple request queue in front of Ollama calls so concurrent agent requests run sequentially with predictable wait times, rather than contending
  - **Done:** Added `_ollama_semaphore = asyncio.Semaphore(OLLAMA_NUM_PARALLEL)` in `app/ollama.py`.
- [x] Make sure timeout logic accounts for queue wait time + generation time, not generation time alone
  - **Done:** Added `calculate_ollama_timeout(num_predict)` in `app/ollama.py` using `(num_predict / measured_tok_per_sec) * TIMEOUT_BUFFER + 10s prompt buffer`.
- [ ] Trim prompt sizes going into the model — summarize/truncate large data payloads (full transaction lists, all insights) before inserting into system prompts; prefill cost is smaller than decode cost but not free

## P2 — Make narrative-generating agents feel responsive

- [ ] For `report`, `insights`, `budget`, `goal`, `alert`, `chart_alert`: return the computed data portion of the response immediately; stream the narrative text via SSE (reuse `/debug/stream` infra) instead of blocking the whole HTTP response on generation
- [ ] Add response caching for narrative text keyed by `(user_id, data_hash, agent)` with a short TTL so repeated/refreshed requests don't re-hit Ollama for identical input
  - **Done:** Added `app/response_cache.py` with `ResponseCache` class and `hash_data()` helper. TTL configurable via `RESPONSE_CACHE_TTL` env var.
- [ ] Add a circuit breaker: if Ollama fails/times out repeatedly in a short window, skip LLM calls and serve data-only responses until it recovers
  - **Done:** Added `app/circuit_breaker.py` with `CircuitBreaker` class and `ollama_circuit_breaker` instance. Integrated into all `generate()`, `stream()`, and `generate_with_tools()` calls in `app/ollama.py`. Configurable via `OLLAMA_CIRCUIT_THRESHOLD` and `OLLAMA_CIRCUIT_COOLDOWN` env vars.

## P2 — Observability

- [x] Instrument LLM call logs (`logging_utils.py:88-104`) with per-call duration and token counts so you can track real tok/s over time, not just anecdotal benchmarks
  - **Done:** Updated `log_llm_call()` in `app/logging_utils.py` to accept `eval_count`, `prompt_eval_count`, `eval_duration_ns`, `prompt_eval_duration_ns` and calculate `gen_tok_per_sec` and `prompt_tok_per_sec`. Added `log_llm_call()` calls in `app/ollama.py` after every successful Ollama response.
- [ ] Add a dashboard or simple log-based report of p50/p95/p99 latency per agent
- [ ] Re-run a concurrency test (2-3 simultaneous requests across different agents) to confirm queueing behavior is predictable under the 1B model

## P3 — Structural cleanup (backlog)

- [x] Resolve `TRANSACTION_QUERY` intent being registered but missing from `AGENT_REGISTRY`
  - **Done:** Added `transaction_query` agent to `AGENT_REGISTRY` with slash command `/query`.
- [x] Separate `alert` and `chart_alert` intents instead of sharing one
  - **Done:** Changed `alert` agent intent from `chart_alert` to `alerts`. Router already handled both intents separately.
- [ ] Consider removing the Ollama call from `answer_database_question` entirely — schema info is static and can likely be templated without an LLM
- [ ] Revisit `classify_intent` — if it's only choosing among 9 fixed labels, a smaller/faster classification approach (even keyword-based) may outperform an LLM call for this specific task

---

## Notes / assumptions to verify

- This plan assumes the 1B model produces acceptable *quality* for narrative tasks (goal/budget/insights explanations) — smaller models can be noticeably worse at nuanced financial reasoning than 3B/4B. Worth doing a quick side-by-side quality check on 3-5 real prompts before fully committing narrative generation to the 1B model, not just a speed check.
- If narrative quality on 1B is unacceptable, a hybrid approach is reasonable: 1B model for filter extraction/classification (speed-critical, low reasoning need), keep a larger model only for final narrative text (quality-critical, latency already tolerated by users on a "generate my report" action).

